# Autonomous citizen progression: technical PRD

## 1. Outcome and scope

Build persistent citizens that independently select achievable work, recover from
failure, earn progression through normal gameplay, and travel to the nearest
suitable reachable hunting area. Demonstrate 500 simultaneously active bot
sessions, including meaningful activity in regions without a human player.

The starting pilot has eight citizens, initially four in Elwynn Forest and four
in Dun Morogh, according to the prior operator session. This document does not
revalidate their current online state. Placement quotas initialize the experiment;
individual progress determines later destinations. Bots need not level together.
The first pilot validates same-map travel: Elwynn Forest and Dun Morogh are both
on map 0. It does not require cross-continent transport.

This is the focused technical successor for autonomous citizens to
[TW-BOTS-001](living-world.prd). Earlier baseline descriptions in that document are
historical. Companion commands retain priority when a citizen is recruited.
This proposal does not revise ownership or earned-character progression rules.

500 active sessions and horizontal planning capacity are separate deliverables.
External planning workers can be replicated; distributing authoritative map/world
simulation requires a separate design and is outside this implementation scope.
Offline identities, decorative NPCs, and fabricated offline XP do not satisfy the
500-active-session target. Capacity and hardware sufficiency remain unmeasured.
500 simultaneous active sessions is the final capacity acceptance gate. Passing
a smaller stage is partial progress and must not mark this requirement complete.

## 2. Verified starting point and known gaps

Source references are paths and symbols because line numbers change during work.
The reviewed worktree extends commits e1a22d5 and 9d6ce50 and is checkpointed at
e5648e9. Record the current implementation revision in each evidence card.

| Evidence | Current behavior | Gap to address |
|---|---|---|
| PlayerBotAI.cpp: TryCitizenDisengage | One candidate toward home; sets a 45-second pause even if movement was not issued | No alternate escape or confirmed separation; moved:0 also means missing/mismatched home or home within four yards |
| PlayerBotMgr.cpp: SelectCitizenProgressionDestination | Uses zone creature level distributions and nearest straight-line anchor | Whole-zone suitability does not prove local target availability, route reachability, or safety |
| PlayerBotAI.cpp: AdvanceProgressionTravel | Short legs toward destination; zone entry OR proximity ends travel | Can finish before reaching a productive hunting area; blocked routes need remembered alternatives |
| PlayerBotAI.cpp: PersistCitizenJournal | Durable progression intent and target | Extend through versioned migrations; preserve restart behavior |
| PlayerBotMgr.cpp: RecordCitizenRouteEdge | Existing route-observation persistence hook | Audit its consumers, validity and bounds before extending it into shared route planning |
| Companion/ConversationTransport.cpp: WorkerLoop | One sender waits for each response outside the world thread | More model-server slots alone do not provide concurrent requests from this caller |
| PlayerBotMgr.cpp: SubmitWorldIntent | Global submission pacing | Fairness, admission budget and queue-age measurement needed for 500 citizens |
| docker/personality-service/combat_review.py | Short advisory reflection from six counters | A generated reflection does not establish adaptive combat policy |

The current progression threshold is 15% of profiled creatures in level-3 through
the citizen's level. Level 60 is a leveling cap. Preserve this conservative
baseline until a measured replacement passes the pilot. Logs from the prior
session reported repeated retreat/reacquisition; the precise live cause remains
unverified. A movement call or moved:1 log does not establish successful travel.

## 3. Architecture and ownership

1. **World executor:** owns live entities, combat legality, movement, rewards and
   persistence. All proposed actions are revalidated here before execution.
2. **Citizen controller:** owns activity state, personal capability estimates,
   failure history, commitments and deterministic fallback selection.
3. **Shared world knowledge:** bounded hunting-area profiles, route graph,
   temporary hazards and expiring area reservations; shared across citizens.
4. **Population scheduler:** budgets strategic work and distributes expensive
   scans/path requests over time. It preserves required session/combat updates.
5. **Planning broker and workers:** consume compact immutable snapshots and return
   expiring proposals. Optional LLM advice cannot block ordinary gameplay.

Extract these responsibilities incrementally from PlayerBotAI/PlayerBotMgr into
focused components. Avoid a wholesale rewrite of working companion behavior.
Every activity has one owner; party recruitment cancels or suspends citizen work
and invalidates outstanding planning requests.

## 4. Requirements

### AC-01: Explicit activity lifecycle and survival (P0)

Represent Hunt, Travel, Rest, Retreat, RecoverCorpse, Maintain, and PartyDuty with
entry conditions, progress signals, completion conditions, deadline, failure
reason and fallback. Select priorities explicitly: immediate survival and party
authority preempt optional work. Model responses never bypass these priorities.

Retreat samples a bounded set of path-valid candidates using threat direction,
other hostiles, known safe ground and escape distance. Track actual displacement
and separation. If no candidate works, attempt a different bounded recovery
action and record failure. Suppress voluntary reacquisition of the failed target
while recovering, without suppressing necessary self-defense. Do not manufacture
successful combat disengagement by clearing bookkeeping alone.

Proposed initial recovery budgets: sample at most eight candidates, check progress
within five seconds, and escalate after three failed attempts. Tune from fixtures;
these are defaults to validate, not established safe constants. Every failure
path must terminate, defer with a reason, or select a different action.

### AC-02: Hunting areas and personal capability (P0)

Build small hunting-area profiles from existing world data and observations:
map/zone, reachable anchor, level distribution, eligible enemy count, respawn
availability, hostile density, service access and observation freshness.
Reject illegal targets before scoring. Keep spatial scans bounded and shared.

Maintain bounded per-bot statistics for kill time, damage taken, resource use,
recovery duration, retreats and deaths by enemy category. Include actual learned
abilities and equipment in capability decisions. Require minimum samples and
confidence before relaxing conservative target selection; decay old evidence and
reset or discount it after material gear, spell or level changes.

Do not infer causes from aggregate counters alone. Failed casts, navigation
failures and combat defeats need distinct categories. No GM boosts or automatic
replacement of earned gear to make a behavior test pass.

### AC-03: Productive independent progression (P0)

Track XP per active minute, eligible-target scarcity, failed pulls and recovery
cost. Distinguish intentional rest, travel and party duty from unexplained idle.
Evaluate local alternatives before leaving a zone. Use observed productivity and
capability in addition to the existing creature distribution gate.

Use minimum dwell time and a required improvement margin to prevent oscillation.
Initial proposal: ten-minute observation windows, two poor windows before normal
relocation, and a 20% predicted improvement for elective switching. Danger and
unreachable routes may interrupt immediately. Tune thresholds using recorded
outcomes; normal differing class/gear performance must remain visible.

At level cap, select explicitly supported maintenance/social/party activities;
do not repeatedly request another leveling destination.

### AC-04: Reachable routes and meaningful arrival (P0)

Filter candidate areas by faction/access rules, capability, usable targets and
capacity. Rank feasible candidates by estimated safe travel time. Use a graph of
verified crossings and waypoints with local pathfinding between nodes. Straight
line distance is only a coarse candidate prefilter.

Arrival requires the expected map/area and verified access to the destination's
usable ground; crossing a zone boundary alone is insufficient. Record attempted
route edges and outcomes. Failed edges receive reason-specific temporary
penalties and bounded backoff. Replanning should try alternatives before retrying
the same failed edge. Terrain/content version changes invalidate stale evidence.
Represent no known route as an explicit result. Cross-continent transport is a
later slice with its own boarding, waiting, arrival and failure tests.

### AC-05: Population distribution and shared learning (P1)

Assign soft area capacity from suitable spawns, respawn supply and competition.
Use expiring reservations, bounded leases and jittered departures. Respect human
access to quest targets. Avoid moving all citizens because one area briefly looks
better. Account for destination reservations when planning travel.

Share coarse successful progression transitions keyed by map and directed
source/destination zones. Count only a citizen's observed walking progress
followed by verified arrival at the selected anchor; zone entry, restart spawn
near the anchor, emergency relocation, patrol travel and failed journeys do not
count. Retain the source zone in the progression journal across restart.

Use shared counts only as a final tie-break after faction/access, productive
prey, anchor safety and that citizen's own path checks pass. The shared hint must
not change the bounded path-query budget or candidate cursor order. Cap its score,
expire evidence after 90 days, and ignore it when its source/content policy
version is stale. Failed journeys remain personal bounded cooldowns and never
decrement or blacklist a shared transition. Do not store movement coordinates in
this population-wide evidence.

### AC-06: Maintenance and continuity (P1)

Plan food/drink, bags, equipment, repairs, training and corpse recovery through
verified services and ordinary game rules. Add each service as a bounded slice.
Persist stable activity intent and necessary progress, with transactional or
idempotent handling appropriate to existing storage. On restart, validate goals
against the current world before resuming. Release stale reservations.

Use new ordered migrations; do not rewrite applied migration files. Prove saved
character, inventory and quest state across an actual restart/restore before
claiming persistence. Never exercise destructive fixtures on personal volumes.

### AC-07: Scheduling and horizontal planning (P1)

Measure existing costs before choosing concurrency or hardware. Event-driven
replanning occurs after level/capability change, repeated failure, destination
loss or activity completion. Stagger periodic strategic refreshes. Cache area
profiles and candidate routes by map, capability band and content version.

Maintain explicit per-tick budgets for discretionary scans and route requests;
defer work fairly when exhausted. Preserve server-required movement, combat and
session processing even for distant citizens. Coalesce dirty journal writes and
rate-limit repetitive diagnostics. Track queue age and starvation.

Horizontal workers receive bot-scoped opaque IDs, snapshot generation, activity
revision, content version, allowed option IDs and deadline. Return request ID,
selected option ID, expiry and bounded reason code. The executor accepts at most
one current result, rejects duplicates/stale revisions, and validates actual
world state. Workers have no live entity pointers or character-database write
authority. A broker lease prevents competing workers owning one request.

Scale workers independently of the authoritative world process. Prove safe
worker loss, duplicate delivery, delayed replies and broker restart before
adding replicas. World simulation sharding is a separate future proposal.

### AC-08: Optional local LLM (P2)

Use the existing local inference service for infrequent selection among validated
strategic options, personality and coordination, and sanitized failure analysis.
Keep combat execution and pathfinding deterministic. Send neither credentials,
raw logs, private character/account records nor transcripts. Runtime prompts use
minimal derived features and opaque option IDs, with a versioned schema.

The model may choose a bounded option and commitment duration. Invalid, expired,
unavailable or unsupported output invokes deterministic fallback. Evaluate its
effect on measured outcomes against the same controller with LLM advice disabled.
Useful dialogue alone does not prove better progression.

Add bounded concurrent requests to the caller only after measuring server
throughput and GPU contention. Use a fair queue, party-interaction priority with
reserved citizen service capacity, timeouts, cancellation, request/token budgets
and a circuit breaker. No retry storm and no paid/cloud fallback.

Sizing example: 500 citizens / 600 seconds = 0.833 requests/sec (50/minute).
The documented 10-second global pacing allows at most six world-intent submits
per minute. For this comparison, one strategic review means one request through
the current world-intent transport; that pacing is an inference submission gate,
not a limit on ordinary world actions. A replacement broker must explicitly
reconcile this gate with its request budget. With average service time L seconds,
the illustrative
required mean concurrency is arrival_rate * L, plus tested headroom. Concurrency
alone cannot overcome insufficient tokens/sec. Choose frequency from measured
capacity and use event-triggered or shared decisions where useful.

### AC-09: Observability and operator controls (P0 throughout)

Expose configured roster, online, active, deferred and offline counts separately.
For each citizen, report activity/reason, last meaningful progress, destination,
failure count and next retry. Aggregate XP rate, death/retreat rates, successful
arrivals, stuck duration and area occupancy without hiding outliers.

Measure p50/p95/p99 world tick duration, AI CPU time, path queue latency, database
write rate/latency, memory, log rate, model throughput, queue age, rejected stale
responses and fallback rate. Sample detailed traces locally with retention caps.
Provide feature flags for new policies and a controlled rollback to deterministic
baseline. Downscale by normal save/logout, never delete character records.

### AC-10: Autonomous hunting-group lifecycle (P0)

Autonomous hunting groups are bot-only parties formed around an active solo
hunt. They are not human party duty; they carry their own bounded lifecycle
and authority policy.

- Formation: bot-only members, same map/zone/team, within 30 yards, level
  difference at most three, out of combat, and each candidate at or above
  the hunt-ready health percentage (initially 80%). Recruitment is bounded
  by group capacity; candidates below any gate stay on solo activity.
- Placement: on ordinary login or configured-zone restart seeding, when a
  citizen has a same-team, same-zone, similarly
  leveled, healthy, ungrouped citizen available, prefer a safe walkable spawn
  8–30 yards from that peer. Preserve the existing 80-yard separation from
  other players and queued spawns; use normal dispersed placement if no safe
  cluster point is available. Apply this to every citizen faction and class.
  This creates opportunities for ordinary hunt formation without forcing
  citizens into a group or across long distances.
- Authority: the leader provides the shared target and travel direction.
  Members keep independent survival authority: a member's own combat,
  retreat, recovery and loot completion always preempt voluntary following
  or assistance. Group assistance is voluntary joining, never forced
  re-engagement, and never bypasses the member's readiness: readiness is
  hunt-ready health plus a free recovery commitment (no rest window, no
  active retreat, no deferred retreat retry and no corpse pending loot),
  and a suppressed failed hunt target is never a group re-engagement
  target.
- Leader loss: when the leader is dead, disconnected or otherwise
  unavailable on the member's map, a threatened member immediately falls
  through to ordinary combat/retreat evaluation without canceling the
  episode: the accumulated age is preserved across self-defense and the
  outcome resolves on the next safe tick. An unthreatened member waits up
  to the regroup deadline (initially sixty seconds, accumulated from tick
  diffs) and then exits to solo citizen activity: disbanding when the
  leader is dead or unavailable, or leaving the group when the leader is
  alive but on another map, where the leader's group keeps running. A
  live same-map leader that the member cannot bring within follow range
  for a bounded follow progress deadline (initially two minutes of
  accumulated unreachable time) also ends in the member leaving the
  group. The wait resets when the leader is usable again; a new group
  identity, death, teleport and genuine party/recovery authority clear it.
- Disband: level spread beyond the formation bound, or an expired leader
  wait. A member may exit safely back to solo activity at any time; leaving
  or disbanding never leaves stale combat or movement state.
- Human recruitment: a human may invite a citizen that is already in an
  autonomous hunting group; the ordinary already-grouped rejection is
  waived for that case and only that case (a human, never a bot session,
  inviting an unowned, unrecruited citizen in the same map and zone). On
  accept the citizen leaves its autonomous group first, its group
  episode state is cleared, and companion command priority takes over for
  recruited duty.
- Restart: group membership is a normal persisted group (the groups and
  group_member tables, loaded at startup like any other group), not a
  runtime-only construct. After restart, members that are online while
  their leader is not wait for the leader under the same bounded
  deadline, then leave or disband and resume solo citizen activity; the
  group persists in the database while absent, and the leader reuses it
  when it next pulls a target instead of forming a duplicate. Partial
  member login must not strand or freeze any member.
- Progress: each member's earned XP and loot are accounted individually;
  group duty never substitutes for per-member progression evidence, and
  autonomous groups are not excluded from population accounting as human
  party duty.

Acceptance: kill or disconnect the leader while a follower is attacked
(assert the follower fights or retreats normally without waiting for the
leader); recruit a member into a human party mid-route (assert companion
priority and safe group exit); let one member rest, lag behind or change
maps (assert bounded separation into solo activity); restart with followers
logging in before the leader (assert bounded regroup deadline, no frozen
members, per-member progress intact). For placement, log in or restart-seed
a same-band citizen beside an eligible peer (assert a walkable point within 30 yards,
80-yard separation from everyone else, then normal group formation after a
pull). The staged zone hunting-group fixture establishes formation and
membership only; these failure paths still need world evidence.

## 5. Delivery and acceptance

| Slice | Deliverables | Required evidence |
|---|---|---|
| S0: baseline | Per-citizen activity/progress metrics, repeatable fixtures, capability flags | Capture eight-bot baseline with model off/on status and source revision |
| S1: recovery | AC-01 and explicit arrival semantics | Missing home, nearby home, blocked escape, repeated threat, interrupted travel; bounded alternate actions and no false success |
| S1G: hunting groups | AC-10 | Formation and membership; leader loss with attacked follower; human recruitment mid-route; member rest/map separation; partial-login restart; per-member progress |
| S2: progression | AC-02/03 and local area selection | Different capabilities progress independently; depleted areas trigger alternatives; stable choices under noisy observations |
| S3: routes/distribution | AC-04/05 | Obstructed nearest destination, safer alternative, capacity contention, stale knowledge and no-route case |
| S4: maintenance | AC-06 | Bags/resources/repair/training/death slices; persistence across restart; recruited-party preemption |
| S5: capacity | AC-07 and stepped population ramp | 25, 50, 100, 250, 500 active sessions; fair distant activity and measured limiting resource |
| S6: LLM and replicas | AC-08 and worker replication | Disabled/stalled/malformed model, stale/duplicate response, worker/broker failure; baseline gameplay continues |

At every slice, run appropriate deterministic tests and inspect the diff. Python
gates: python -m unittest discover -s docker -p 'test_*.py'; compile changed Python;
parse changed PowerShell; validate Compose and git diff --check. C++ changes
require a server build with the existing BUILD_JOBS value and Turtle addons on.
Database-dependent behavior needs disposable fixtures. Source-text assertions
and a clean build do not prove successful gameplay.

Pilot acceptance requires observed combat/XP, loot/equipment, death/recovery,
normal travel and restart persistence. Test quest completion only for declared
supported quest types and record unsupported content explicitly. All eight must
show independent meaningful progress or an explicit bounded defer reason; no
bot may remain in an unnoticed repeat-failure loop.
S1 must distinguish the actual retreat failure reason using bounded local
observations before declaring the live symptom fixed; reacquisition suppression
alone is insufficient evidence.

Proposed measurable distant-activity floors: in each 30-minute load interval,
at least 90% of available autonomous citizens in human-free zones must complete
one legitimate gameplay objective (successful hunt/loot, maintenance interaction,
or confirmed travel arrival). Track recovery-only citizens separately. Detect
unexplained no-progress within 60 seconds and begin a bounded recovery or report
an explicit blocked reason within another 60 seconds. Any citizen remaining
blocked for five minutes fails the stage until investigated. Exclude party duty
and intentional operator pauses explicitly, not by silently removing outliers.
Commit the final floors before S5; these proposed values require S0 calibration.

For each load stage, run a proposed minimum 30-minute steady interval after login
stabilizes, then a proposed two-hour soak at the highest passing stage. Before
ramping, record numeric tick/path/database/queue budgets from S0 and commit them
to the acceptance evidence; do not choose favorable limits after the result.
Require zero crashes, bounded queues/memory, no unexplained stuck citizens and
continued activity without humans present. Compare individual progression and
failure distributions to the eight-bot baseline. Longer gameplay observation is
still needed to establish full leveling-journey correctness.

## 6. Risks and open decisions

- Establish the actual server tick budget and sustainable local-model throughput.
- Audit existing route/danger memory consumers before adding overlapping tables.
- Determine minimum reachable enemy supply for an area and appropriate per-class
  capability sample counts using fixtures; avoid arbitrary universal constants.
- Separate broken world content from navigation and policy defects in reports.
- Agree cross-map transport scope after same-map progression passes.
- If 500 active sessions exceed capacity, report the highest passing population
  and measured bottleneck. Preserve the original target in subsequent planning.

## 7. Implementation workflow

Use retrieval scoped to tortoise-wow, verify current source/hashes, and give one
fresh bounded local worker an eligible task per implementation slice. Keep
evidence and sanitized worker reports in ignored local/. Hosted Codex owns
architecture, source review, validation and acceptance. Sync changed tracked
source after validation and report embedding/derived-write counts.

This PRD records proposed work. The checkpoint preserves the pilot with known
recovery limitations; it is not a claim of 500-bot readiness or a deployment.

## 8. Implementation progress (2026-09-27)

S0/S1 are in progress; neither slice has final gameplay acceptance.

- Recovery now has an explicit value policy: actual displacement plus natural
  combat clearance is required for escape, five-second movement checks detect
  stalls, and a fifteen-second lifetime bounds an attempt. Rest cannot cancel
  an active retreat. Failed candidate selection leaves self-defense available.
- The world adapter tries eight bounded escape candidates, checks nearby hostile
  clearance, and reports start, escaped, stalled, timed-out and unavailable
  outcomes. Monitor counts distinguish these outcomes from attempted movement.
  The first live window showed thirty-yard movement with combat retained. Core
  Creature::IsOutOfThreatArea uses a configurable radius (default fifty yards)
  and a twelve-second leash-extension delay. Candidate distance now derives
  from that radius with a bounded margin, and direction uses combat-start
  position. This adjustment still requires measured escape outcomes.
- Arrival requires the expected map and zone, a finite nonnegative distance
  inside twelve yards, and verified access to the selected anchor. An unusable
  anchor or zone entry alone cannot record successful arrival. Clipped walk
  endpoints must still reach the anchor within two yards in the expected zone.
- Qwen implemented the recovery value helper/tests and initial arrival policy.
  Hosted review found and corrected arrival acceptance for wrong-zone, invalid
  distance and unusable-ground cases. New behavior tests cover those failures.
- The earlier full suite reported 392 tests with 8 failures, 7 setup errors,
  3 skips and 1 expected failure. These are unresolved; assertions about load
  flakiness are not accepted as a diagnosis. The companion-dismissal failure
  also reproduced in a focused fixture before the reviewed arrival changes.
- Remaining S1 work includes reproducible world fixtures for blocked escape,
  multiple threats, interruption/party preemption, actual safe arrival and
  restart continuity; repeated-failure escalation needs further validation.
  The new helper tests and build do not substitute for these world fixtures.
- Resume pass (2026-09-27, park-head local session): the three findings
  from the S1 integration review (card v3) were re-verified against
  current source; the card's recorded SHA256 no longer matches the file,
  so line references were re-located rather than trusted. Finding 1
  (same-map teleport leaving an active retreat with a stale destination)
  was already corrected in-tree. Finding 2 (the interrupt/death/party
  reset clears retreat state but not the pending retreat motion leg) and
  finding 3 (unguarded me->GetSession() in the teleport-ack block) were
  fixed: the reset path now clears the motion master symmetrically with
  the terminal retreat outcomes, and both teleport acks are
  session-guarded. ByteBuffer::append auto-resizes on write, so the
  ten-byte ack packet writing sixteen bytes is safe. Rebuilt
  tortoise-local:dev with BUILD_JOBS=2; clean compile.
- Companion-dismissal bench failure root cause: docker/test_bot_bench.py
  still asserted the pre-KAP-558 "dismiss benches the bot offline"
  contract, while the current documented contract (companion-commands.md)
  is "stays online, released from party orders, roams locally". The test
  was updated to the current contract (companion online stays 1 across
  reconciliation samples and leaves the party group in every sample);
  the bench fixture passes 6/6 on the rebuilt image.

Private validation captures remain under ignored local/prd-s1/. Do not copy
raw runtime logs or character snapshots into retrieval or model assignments.

### Shared migration hints (2026-10-01)

The `feature/shared-citizen-migration-hints` slice adds versioned, expiring
map/source-zone/destination-zone evidence. It is loaded into an in-memory cache
once and updated on a verified, walked progression arrival. Each citizen still
passes the existing suitability, personal blocked-anchor and bounded navmesh
checks. Evidence only breaks ties after estimated travel distance and level fit;
the candidate cursor and eight-query limit are unchanged. Source zone is stored
in the durable progression journal so travel can resume across zone entry and
restart. An arrival after restart without observed walking progress completes
the citizen's route but does not teach the shared hint. This is fixture-level
implementation evidence; live progression observations and full-suite gates
remain separate acceptance work.

### S1 follow-up (2026-09-28)

The latest complete pre-follow-up suite capture ran 383 tests with 6 failures,
9 setup errors, 3 skips and 1 expected failure. Later focused captures passed
bench 6/6, defend 11/11 and independent-value 9/9; these do not establish a
green full suite. The local changes also include regroup/equipment fixture
placement corrections, the online recall/dismiss party contract, and Hold
movement cancellation. Their existence does not close the remaining suite
failures or S1 gameplay acceptance.

The next bounded recovery slice adds failure escalation: the first two failed
attempts retry after five seconds; the third and subsequent failures defer for
thirty seconds while ordinary self-defense remains available. Failed escape
endpoints are remembered in at most three entries for sixty seconds; candidates
within eight yards are excluded so alternatives are considered. Success,
genuine out-of-combat health recovery, death, teleport or party authority clears
the episode. These are initial policy constants, not calibrated gameplay claims.
The world adapter logs failure reason, capped failure count and retry delay;
the monitor counts defer events separately from successful escapes.

Validation for this slice is recorded under ignored local/prd-s1-escalation/.

- Server build passed with BUILD_JOBS=2 and Turtle addons enabled.
- Recovery and arrival value runners passed with a real C++ compiler; existing
  independent-value checks passed 9/9 and placement checks passed 6/6.
- The new disposable recovery world fixture passed: a normal hunt reaches low
  health; a crowded escape scan produces failures one and two with five-second
  retries, failure three with a thirty-second defer, continued self-defense
  during that defer, and a fourth failure retaining the longer delay. No escape
  or movement success is fabricated. Personal container identities/start times
  were unchanged. Earlier fixture attempts did not establish combat and are
  setup failures, not evidence for or against the recovery policy.
- The current party lifecycle fixture passed 6/6 on the rebuilt image.
- The full suite was not rerun. Successful escape paths, blocked movement,
  multiple active attackers, interruption during a retreat and restart
  continuity still require their own world evidence. The passing crowded-scan
  fixture does not establish those behaviors or calibrated survival outcomes.

S0/S1 remain open until the required gameplay, path, interruption and continuity
evidence is complete. The recovery slice is now running on the personal server
for operator review (2026-09-28). Before restart, 25 ZoneCitizenAI characters
were online; the final rollout check found zero human characters online. The
world container uses candidate image
sha256:5d243e54fd23cc6ae8680bd46451bf12dd23bb168816b6ae9019559e1ddf23f5. A
pre-restart database backup was saved as
local/backups/tortoise-20260928T143504Z.sql. Restart completed without fatal
or error log matches. No personal data volume or schema was reset. Real-player
review must now establish a natural retreat outcome in the live world; the
synthetic crowded-scan fixture does not provide that evidence.

### S1G hunting-group lifecycle (2026-09-28)

AC-10 landed with two priority fixes to the previously uncommitted group
code, addressing the robustness review's group findings:

- Leader loss no longer suspends a follower's own survival. A dead or
  unavailable leader makes a threatened follower fall straight through to
  ordinary combat/retreat evaluation; an unthreatened follower waits up to
  a bounded sixty-second regroup deadline and then disbands and resumes
  solo activity. The decision is the value policy
  Companion::CitizenRecovery::EvaluateLeaderLoss with
  kLeaderLostDeadlineMs; the wait accumulates from tick diffs, saturates
  at the deadline and resets whenever the leader is usable again.
- Voluntary combat joining now requires hunt-ready health.
  JoinCitizenCombat and group formation both gate on
  PlayerBotAI::CitizenCombatReady (initially 80% of max health), the same
  readiness the solo hunt pull requires, now shared through the constant
  kHuntReadyHealthPercent. Already-engaged citizens are unaffected:
  necessary self-defense keeps running through the ordinary combat path.

Value tests cover the leader-loss decision (threatened fall-through at all
wait times, wait/disband deadline boundary, saturated input) and the
follow-stall deadline bounds. The incremental server build of this slice
passed in the existing Docker build image; the AC-10 world fixtures
(attacked follower under a dead leader, human recruitment of a grouped
citizen, cross-map separation, same-map follow stall, partial-login
restart) are still outstanding, and the staged zone hunting-group fixture
proves formation and membership only.

A follow-up bolster pass closed the remaining AC-10 review findings: the
human takeover of a grouped citizen (the already-grouped invite rejection
waived only for that case, with an explicit group transfer on accept); a
live cross-map leader entering the bounded leader-loss episode and
expiring into the follower leaving the group; the same-map follow
progress deadline; combat readiness including a free recovery commitment
and failed-target suppression; leader-loss episode age surviving
threatened fall-through with resets limited to a new group identity,
death, teleport or genuine party/recovery authority; and the gear-roll
marker written only after a synchronous inventory save, with failed equip
or marker writes logged and resolving as a harmless deterministic
re-roll. The restart bullet above was corrected: these are normal
persisted groups, not runtime-only membership.
